  <p>
    Every physical location on Earth—from the peak of Mount Everest to your living room—can be uniquely expressed using two numbers: <strong>latitude (φ)</strong> and <strong>longitude (λ)</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Together, these angular coordinates form the universal grid of global cartography, geodesy, marine navigation, and modern web mapping engines like Google Maps, OpenStreetMap, and Leaflet. In this guide, we break down how coordinates work, how to convert between standards with our <a href="/tools/coordinate-converter/">Coordinate Converter</a>, and how many decimal places you truly need.
  </p>

