  <h2>1. Understanding Latitude (The Parallels)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Latitude (φ)</strong> measures how far north or south a location is relative to the Earth's <strong>Equator</strong>. Lines of latitude run horizontally east-west around the globe and are called <em>parallels</em> because they remain equidistant from one another at all points.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Equator (0°):</strong> The fundamental baseline dividing Earth into the Northern (+ / N) and Southern (- / S) Hemispheres.</li>
    <li><strong>North Pole (+90° N):</strong> The northernmost rotational axis of the planet.</li>
    <li><strong>South Pole (-90° S):</strong> The southernmost rotational axis of the planet.</li>
    <li><strong>Tropic of Cancer (+23.4365° N):</strong> The northernmost latitude where the Sun can appear directly overhead at solar noon.</li>
    <li><strong>Tropic of Capricorn (-23.4365° S):</strong> The southernmost latitude where the Sun can appear directly overhead.</li>
    <li><strong>Arctic Circle (+66.56° N) & Antarctic Circle (-66.56° S):</strong> The boundaries of polar day and polar night.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Distance per Degree of Latitude</h3>
  <p>
    Because latitude lines are parallel, <strong>1 degree of latitude is approximately 111 kilometers (69 miles)</strong> everywhere on Earth, varying slightly from 110.57 km at the Equator to 111.69 km at the poles due to Earth's slight polar flattening.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Understanding Longitude (The Meridians)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Longitude (λ)</strong> measures how far east or west a location is from the designated <strong>Prime Meridian</strong>. Lines of longitude run vertically from pole to pole and are called <em>meridians</em>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The Prime Meridian (0°):</strong> Established internationally at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London (1884).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Eastern Hemisphere (0° to +180° E):</strong> Stretches east across Europe, Asia, Africa, and Oceania. Expressed as positive values in decimal notation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Western Hemisphere (0° to -180° W):</strong> Stretches west across the Atlantic and the Americas. Expressed as negative values in decimal notation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The 180th Meridian (Antimeridian):</strong> Lies directly opposite the Prime Meridian in the Pacific Ocean, roughly coinciding with the International Date Line.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Distance per Degree of Longitude Shrinks Near the Poles</h3>
  <p>
    Unlike latitude, meridians converge at both poles. The ground distance of 1 degree of longitude depends strictly on your current latitude:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Distance per 1° Longitude (ΔL)</strong> ≈ <code>111.32 km × cos(Latitude)</code>
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li>At the Equator (0°): <strong>111.32 km</strong> (69.17 miles)</li>
    <li>At Paris / Seattle (48° N): <strong>74.6 km</strong> (46.3 miles)</li>
    <li>At London (51.5° N): <strong>69.3 km</strong> (43.0 miles)</li>
    <li>At the North Pole (90° N): <strong>0 km</strong> (all meridians converge into a single point)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Coordinate Formats: Decimal Degrees vs DMS vs UTM</h2>
  <p>
    Geographic coordinates are commonly recorded in two primary human-readable formats and one metric projected grid system:
  </p>

  <div class="step-container">
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">DD</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Decimal Degrees (DD) — The Web & GPS Standard</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Used by all modern software, JavaScript APIs, and GPS devices. Positive indicates North/East; negative indicates South/West.<br />
          <em>Example:</em> <code>40.7128, -74.0060</code> (New York City Hall)
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">DMS</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Degrees, Minutes, Seconds (DMS) — The Navigational Standard</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          The sexagesimal system dividing 1 degree into 60 minutes (') and 1 minute into 60 seconds ("). Used in aviation, marine charting, and military operations.<br />
          <em>Example:</em> <code>40° 42' 46.1" N, 74° 00' 21.6" W</code>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">UTM</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) — The Metric Grid</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Divides the world into 60 longitudinal zones of 6° each, measuring distance in meters (Easting and Northing) rather than spherical angles. Explore our dedicated <a href="/guides/utm-coordinates-and-datums/">UTM Coordinates Guide</a>.<br />
          <em>Example:</em> <code>Zone 18T, Easting 583960m E, Northing 4507350m N</code>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>4. How Many Decimal Places Do You Actually Need?</h2>
  <p>
    In web development and GIS data storage, storing excessive decimal places consumes memory and gives a false illusion of precision. Here is the official WGS 84 precision scale:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Decimal Places</th>
        <th>Degree Fraction</th>
        <th>Ground Precision (at Equator)</th>
        <th>Scale & Real-World Use Case</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>0</strong></td>
        <td>1.0°</td>
        <td>~ 111 km (69 miles)</td>
        <td>Country or large continent region</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>1</strong></td>
        <td>0.1°</td>
        <td>~ 11.1 km (6.9 miles)</td>
        <td>Large metropolitan city area</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2</strong></td>
        <td>0.01°</td>
        <td>~ 1.11 km (0.69 miles)</td>
        <td>Town, district, village, or large park</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>3</strong></td>
        <td>0.001°</td>
        <td>~ 111 meters (364 ft)</td>
        <td>Large agricultural parcel, airport runway</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>4</strong></td>
        <td>0.0001°</td>
        <td>~ 11.1 meters (36.4 ft)</td>
        <td>Individual house parcel, street identification</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>5</strong></td>
        <td>0.00001°</td>
        <td>~ 1.11 meters (3.64 ft)</td>
        <td>Doorway entrance, lane-level smartphone GPS</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>6</strong></td>
        <td>0.000001°</td>
        <td>~ 11.1 centimeters (4.37 in)</td>
        <td>Surveyor marker, civil engineering stake</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>7</strong></td>
        <td>0.0000001°</td>
        <td>~ 1.11 centimeters (0.44 in)</td>
        <td>Tectonic plate motion monitoring</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>Rule of Thumb:</strong> For all general web mapping, address lookups, and mobile navigation, <strong>5 or 6 decimal places</strong> provide the ultimate balance between sub-meter accuracy and data efficiency.
  </blockquote>

